---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
---
<BaseLayout
  title="Guides"
  description="Practical Lowcountry Digital Works guides on website ownership, handoff, maintenance, and operating digital systems without unnecessary vendor dependence."
>
  <section class="page-hero page-hero--warm">
    <div class="container readable">
      <p class="eyebrow">Guides & field notes</p>
      <h1>Practical guidance for owning and operating your digital systems.</h1>
      <p class="lede">These are small, first-hand guides based on how LDW approaches real delivery work: keep ownership understandable, maintain the paths the business actually depends on, and add technology only when it solves a real problem.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="container">
      <div class="section-heading">
        <p class="eyebrow">Current guides</p>
        <h2>Understand what should stay under control after a website goes live.</h2>
        <p class="lede">The first two field notes cover two recurring owner questions: what the business should control, and what ongoing website maintenance should actually check.</p>
      </div>

      <div class="grid grid--2 guide-index-grid">
        <article class="card card--accent guide-card">
          <p class="card__kicker">Ownership & handoff</p>
          <h2>Website Ownership & Handoff: What Your Business Should Control</h2>
          <p>Domain registration, DNS, hosting, source, recovery, search properties, provider access, recurring services, and what a useful handoff should leave behind.</p>
          <p class="guide-card__meta">Reviewed September 29, 2026</p>
          <a class="text-link" href="/guides/website-ownership-handoff/">Read the ownership guide →</a>
        </article>

        <article class="card card--accent guide-card">
          <p class="card__kicker">Website care</p>
          <h2>Website Maintenance After Launch: What Should Actually Be Checked?</h2>
          <p>Critical customer journeys, content accuracy, accessibility, performance, search integrity, security, recovery, renewals, and how maintenance changes with the stack.</p>
          <p class="guide-card__meta">Reviewed September 29, 2026</p>
          <a class="text-link" href="/guides/website-maintenance-after-launch/">Read the maintenance guide →</a>
        </article>
      </div>

      <aside class="card guide-index-note" style="margin-top: 1.25rem;">
        <p class="card__kicker">Small by design</p>
        <h2>No publishing treadmill.</h2>
        <p>LDW does not need a high-volume blog. New guides should exist because there is a useful, experience-backed answer worth maintaining—not because a content calendar needs another post.</p>
        <a class="text-link" href="/approach/">See the LDW approach →</a>
      </aside>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section section--navy">
    <div class="container final-cta final-cta--single">
      <div>
        <p class="eyebrow">Not sure what needs attention?</p>
        <h2>Start with the part of the website or account model that feels unclear.</h2>
        <p class="lede">Ownership, recovery, outdated information, broken lead paths, accessibility, performance, recurring care, or a provider transition are all reasonable starting points.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="actions">
        <a class="button button--secondary" href="/services/website-care/">Website Care</a>
        <a class="button button--secondary" href="/services/digital-ownership-platform-administration/">Digital Ownership</a>
        <a class="button button--secondary" href="/connect/">Talk with Eddie</a>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>
